From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Egor Kulikovskiy <xianel0x@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:30:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] x9: opt-in early PSCI reset so a hung kernel still leaves a
 pstore record

The X9 has no UART and no working serial console. The only readback channel
is the ramoops console zone, which Android exposes as
/sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops-0 on the *next* boot. That record only
survives a warm reset: DRAM keeps its contents across PSCI SYSTEM_RESET, but
a cold reset (power button / battery) reinitializes it and the log is gone
before Android can snapshot it.

A candidate that hangs after the jump therefore produces no evidence at all:
the device owner has to cold-reset it to get back to Android, which destroys
exactly the record we need. Observed on hardware: the diagnostic stub, which
ends in its own PSCI reset, leaves a readable record; a real-kernel candidate
that hangs and is force-restarted leaves none.

Add a compile-time opt-in that resets from the kernel itself once the pstore
record is mapped and the buffered pre-VM log has been flushed into it. The
reset is deliberately after that flush, so the record always contains the
board-identification tag, the boot_args geometry and every sweet_early_mark
stage that ran. If the kernel hangs after this point the record is already
durable; if it would have booted, the diagnostic build resets instead and the
log is still readable.

Off by default. On a working kernel it turns every boot into a reset loop.
---
 osfmk/arm64/sweet_early.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+)

diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/sweet_early.c b/osfmk/arm64/sweet_early.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/sweet_early.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sweet_early.c
@@ -24,6 +24,20 @@
 static volatile uint32_t *x9_wdt;
 static const char x9_kernel_tag[] __attribute__((used)) = "HelioDarwin-X9-0186\n";
 
+/*
+ * Bring-up aid: reset once the persistent record is durable, instead of
+ * running on. A candidate that hangs after the jump can only be recovered by
+ * a cold reset, which destroys the ramoops record before Android can snapshot
+ * it; resetting here makes the record survive a warm reset instead. The X9
+ * has no UART, so this is the only way to see how far a hung kernel got.
+ * Off by default: on a working kernel it turns every boot into a reset loop.
+ */
+#ifndef SWEET_X9_DIAG_RESET
+#define SWEET_X9_DIAG_RESET 0
+#endif
+
+#define PSCI_SYSTEM_RESET 0x84000009u
+
 /* persistent_ram header: signature, next write offset, valid byte count. */
 #define PSTORE_SIG 0x43474244u
 #define PSTORE_HDR 12u
@@ -270,6 +284,26 @@
 	__atomic_store_n(&pstore, hdr, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
 	/* Called on the boot CPU with interrupts masked, before SMP startup. */
 	sweet_pstore_append(early_log, (int)early_log_size);
+
+#if SWEET_X9_DIAG_RESET
+	/*
+	 * Everything buffered before the mapping is now in DRAM, so the record
+	 * survives the reset below. Announce the reset first: its presence in
+	 * the next boot's record is what distinguishes "we reset on purpose"
+	 * from "the kernel died somewhere after this point".
+	 */
+	sweet_pstore_append("diag: pstore durable, requesting PSCI reset\n",
+	    sizeof("diag: pstore durable, requesting PSCI reset\n") - 1);
+	__asm__ volatile("dsb sy" ::: "memory");
+	{
+		register uint64_t x0 __asm__("x0") = PSCI_SYSTEM_RESET;
+		__asm__ volatile("smc #0" : "+r"(x0) ::
+		    "x1", "x2", "x3", "x4", "x5", "x6", "x7", "x8", "x9",
+		    "x10", "x11", "x12", "x13", "x14", "x15", "x16", "x17",
+		    "memory");
+	}
+	/* If the monitor refuses the call, fall through and keep booting. */
+#endif
 }
 #define EARLY_FB early_fb
 #define EARLY_WIDTH early_width
